---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { site } from "../../data/site";

// Stand-alone privacy policy for the mobile apps we publish on Google Play.
// This is the URL entered in Play Console's store listing, so it must be a
// complete policy on its own. The website has its own policy at /privacy
// (it runs analytics; this doesn't). Describes the OPTIONAL Google Play Games
// Services sign-in added by the Leaderboards & Share release of Delve, Die,
// Repeat (mazeworld .planning/proposed-milestone-leaderboards.md). When that
// build ships, update the Play Console Data safety form to match the "Data
// safety summary" section below (User IDs + App activity, optional, shared
// with Google, for app functionality).
const effective = "September 17, 2026";
// The address Google Play lists as the developer contact. Change here only.
const contact = "sheibeck@gmail.com";
---
<Layout
  title="Privacy Policy — Google Play apps"
  description="Privacy policy for Darktier Studios apps on Google Play, including Delve, Die, Repeat. Saves stay on your device; optional Google Play Games sign-in powers leaderboards."
>
  <div class="container">
    <section style="padding:84px 0 28px">
      <p class="kicker">Darktier Studios · legal · Google Play apps</p>
      <h1 style="font-size:clamp(40px,6vw,72px);line-height:1.04;letter-spacing:-0.02em;margin:0 0 0 -0.06em">Privacy policy for our apps</h1>
      <p class="privacy-meta">Effective {effective} · {site.legalName}, {site.location}</p>
    </section>

    <article class="privacy">
      <p class="privacy-lede">
        The short version: <strong>our apps don't collect your data, and we run no servers.</strong> Your runs,
        your graveyard, and your settings live on your device. The one exception is entirely your call:
        if you choose to sign in with <strong>Google Play Games</strong> to compete on leaderboards, your
        scores and Play Games profile go to Google so other players can see where you placed. Skip it,
        and what happens in the dungeon stays in the dungeon.
      </p>

      <h2>Who this covers</h2>
      <p>
        This policy applies to the mobile apps published by {site.legalName} on Google Play — currently
        <strong>Delve, Die, Repeat</strong> (package <code>com.darktierstudios.delvedierepeat</code>).
        Our website and browser-based tools are covered separately by the
        <a href="/privacy">website privacy policy</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Data we collect</h2>
      <p>
        We — {site.legalName} — collect nothing. We operate no servers, no accounts of our own, and no
        analytics. Our apps do not collect, transmit, sell, or share:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Personal information (name, email address, phone number, address)</li>
        <li>Location data, precise or approximate</li>
        <li>Financial or payment information</li>
        <li>Contacts, calendar, photos, videos, audio, or files</li>
        <li>Gameplay analytics, or crash and diagnostic reports</li>
        <li>Device identifiers or the Android Advertising ID</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The only data that ever leaves your device is the leaderboard data described in the next section,
        and only if you opt in.
      </p>

      <h2>Leaderboards and Google Play Games (optional)</h2>
      <p>
        Delve, Die, Repeat can post your results to <strong>leaderboards</strong> — deepest run, fewest
        steps, most deaths, and the like — so you can compete with other players and with your friends.
        This is powered by <strong>Google Play Games Services</strong>, a feature of Google Play, and it
        works only if you sign in.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Signing in is optional.</strong> The game is fully playable without it. Android may offer
          to sign you in automatically with the Google account already on your phone; you can decline,
          and you can sign out at any time from the account menu in the app. Signed out, no leaderboard
          data is sent anywhere.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What is sent when you're signed in:</strong> your Google Play Games player ID and, for
          each run that ends, the leaderboard score for that run — dungeon depth reached, steps taken,
          your adventurer's race and class, your total death count, and the game version or season the run
          was played under. Your adventurer's name and epitaph are <em>not</em> sent; they stay in your
          local graveyard.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Where it goes:</strong> to Google, not to us. Google stores it and displays it on the
          game's leaderboards under your Play Games profile name and avatar. It is visible to other players
          of the game, and to your Play Games friends if you use that feature. We have no server of our own
          and never receive a copy.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Google's rules apply.</strong> Play Games Services, your Play Games profile, and your
          friends list are governed by
          <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google's privacy policy</a>
          and your <a href="https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/3129346" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Play Games profile settings</a>,
          where you control who can see your profile and activity.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Deleting it:</strong> you can remove your Play Games data for this game at any time from the
          Google Play Games app (Settings → "Delete Play Games account &amp; data" for the game). That erases
          your scores from the leaderboards. Signing out in the app stops any further submissions.
          Step-by-step instructions: <a href="/privacy/delete-data">delete your data</a>.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Sharing your results</h2>
      <p>
        The app can build a shareable card of a fallen adventurer (their epitaph, depth, and a link to the
        game) and hand it to Android's share sheet. Nothing is sent until you pick an app to share it with,
        and it goes only where you send it. We don't see what you share or whom you share it with.
      </p>

      <h2>Data stored on your device</h2>
      <p>
        The game keeps a small amount of data on your phone so it works: your current run, the graveyard of
        adventurers who came before it, your personal best records, and your settings. This data is never
        sent to us, is not part of what goes to Google Play Games (only the scores listed above are), and is
        removed when you uninstall the app.
      </p>

      <h2>No servers, no ads, no third parties beyond Google</h2>
      <p>
        Our apps require no account of our own and work fully offline. They contain no advertising,
        analytics, or crash-reporting SDKs. The only third party involved is Google: Google Play handles your
        purchase and installation, and Google Play Games Services handles the optional leaderboards, both
        under <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google's privacy policy</a>.
        We receive no personal information from either.
      </p>

      <h2>Permissions</h2>
      <p>
        Our apps do not request access to your location, contacts, camera, microphone, storage, or any other
        permission-gated data. Leaderboards use the internet only to talk to Google Play Games when you are
        signed in.
      </p>

      <h2>Children</h2>
      <p>
        Our apps are suitable for general audiences. We collect no data from anyone, including children
        under 13. Signing in to Google Play Games requires a Google account, and Google's own age rules and
        parental controls govern whether a child can create a Play Games profile or appear on leaderboards;
        the game does not need a sign-in to be played.
      </p>

      <h2>Your rights</h2>
      <p>
        We hold no personal data about you, so there is nothing for us to export, correct, or erase.
        Everything the app stores on its own lives on your device under your control; uninstalling the app
        deletes it. Leaderboard data you chose to submit is held by Google and can be reviewed or deleted
        through your Google Play Games settings as described above. If a future app ever changes any of
        this, this policy will change first.
      </p>

      <h2>Data safety summary</h2>
      <p>
        For the Google Play Data safety section, our apps declare: <strong>User IDs</strong> (Play Games
        player ID) and <strong>App activity</strong> (gameplay scores) — collected only if you opt in to
        leaderboards, used for app functionality, shared with Google Play Games Services, encrypted in
        transit, and deletable by you. Nothing else is collected. No data is sold.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
      <p>
        If we update this policy, the new version will be posted at this address with a new effective date.
        Material changes to what an app collects will also be reflected in that app's Google Play listing.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions about this policy: <a href={`mailto:${contact}`}>{contact}</a><br />
        {site.legalName} · {site.location}
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>
